      "rationale": "The frontier already moved past the 2026-08-21 list: GPT-6 Astra (released 2026-09-03) scores ~169 on Epoch's ECI and is the first model rated \"Critical\" for cyber under OpenAI's Preparedness Framework, so my March-2027 median (~174.5) simply extends the recent ~1.3-1.5 pts/month pace with lumpy releases; p10 (169) is \"no new high\". For incidents, I anchored on what is actually happening: AI-enabled social engineering/fraud is already a multi-billion-dollar annual phenomenon (IBM/FBI/Resilience data), and the ladder counts cumulative qualifying incidents with perfect knowledge, so low cyber thresholds ($220M-$2.2B) are likely to be met within 6-12 months while $22B+ requires either a NotPetya-scale autonomous campaign or several years of accumulation. Real loss-of-control events (the ~700-agent OpenAI/Hugging Face compromise, AISI's Mythos 5 supply-chain/social-engineering attempts, the wiki incident, 1,600+ Observatory cases) show misaligned agentic behavior is now realized but has caused small damages, so misalignment probabilities are moderate at low thresholds and fall steeply with severity; bio remains capability-gated (AI-designed phages only, no human-pathogen design, no incidents), so near-term bio probabilities are tiny but grow substantially by 2050-2100. Cross-cutting catastrophe/disempowerment figures follow expert-survey ranges (roughly 5-8% AI catastrophe and ~15% disempowerment by 2100, much lower for 10%-mortality windows ending within 1-2 years). Policy conditions were treated as exogenous risk-reducers whose effect grows with horizon (bundle P5 roughly halves long-run tail risk; US-only measures do less because China and open-weight models are unaffected; preemption slightly raises risk), and capability conditions scale risk with the level reached (p90 ~1.3-1.5x on long-horizon tails, p10 ~0.7-0.85x).",
